{"y":1990,"label":"1989–90","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":31,"l":6,"cw":12,"cl":4,"confFinish":"2nd in Big East","finish":"elite8","seed":1,"region":"East","apHigh":4,"apFinal":4,"srs":18.3,"sos":7.0,"ortg":102.6,"drtg":85.8,"ppg":79.1,"oppg":66.1,"story":{"headline":"The Dream Season","text":"Unranked in the preseason, the 1989–90 Huskies became the team that put UConn basketball on the national map. Led by Chris Smith, Israeli freshman Nadav Henefeld, Scott Burrell, Tate George and Rod Sellers, UConn beat No. 5 Syracuse and No. 2 Georgetown in back-to-back games at the Hartford Civic Center in January and cracked the AP poll for the first time that season. Days later the new Gampel Pavilion opened, and the Huskies christened it with a 72–58 win over No. 15 St. John's on January 27. UConn won a share of the Big East regular-season title, then beat Seton Hall, Georgetown and Syracuse (78–75) at Madison Square Garden for its first Big East tournament crown. As a No. 1 seed in the East, UConn routed Boston University and California before Burrell's full-court pass found George for a buzzer-beater that beat Clemson 71–70 — forever known in Connecticut as \"The Shot.\" Two days later, Duke ended the dream on a buzzer-beater of its own, 79–78 in overtime. UConn finished 31–6, and Calhoun was the consensus national coach of the year.","moments":[{"date":"1990-01-27","title":"Gampel Pavilion opens","text":"UConn beats No. 15 St. John's 72–58 in the first game at Gampel Pavilion."},{"date":"1990-03-11","title":"Big East tournament final: W vs Syracuse","text":"UConn beat Syracuse 78–75 in the Big East tournament final at Madison Square Garden, New York City, New York."},{"date":"1990-03-15","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Boston University","text":"UConn beat Boston University 76–52 in the NCAA tournament first round at Hartford Civic Center, Hartford, Connecticut."},{"date":"1990-03-17","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs California","text":"UConn beat California 74–54 in the NCAA tournament second round at Hartford Civic Center, Hartford, Connecticut."},{"date":"1990-03-22","title":"\"The Shot\"","text":"Scott Burrell's full-court pass finds Tate George, whose buzzer-beater beats Clemson 71–70 in the Sweet Sixteen."},{"date":"1990-03-24","title":"NCAA Elite Eight: L vs Duke","text":"UConn lost to Duke 78–79 in overtime in the NCAA tournament Elite Eight at Brendan Byrne Arena, East Rutherford, New Jersey."}],"honors":["Big East tournament champions","Big East regular-season Co-Champions","Jim Calhoun – AP National Coach of the Year","Jim Calhoun – Sporting News Coach of the Year","Jim Calhoun – Big East Coach of the Year","Nadav Henefeld – Big East Rookie/Freshman of the Year","Chris Smith – Big East Tournament MVP"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1989%E2%80%9390_Connecticut_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Calhoun","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tate_George","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_A._Gampel_Pavilion","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nadav_Henefeld","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scott_Burrell"]},"roster":[{"pid":"chris-smith-1","name":"Chris Smith","num":"13","cls":"SO","pos":"G","ht":"6-3","wt":190,"home":"Bridgeport, CT","photo":"assets/players/chris-smith-1.jpg?v=8bae52c1","photoWide":true,"pg":{"g":37,"gs":37,"mp":33.0,"pts":17.2,"trb":2.5,"ast":3.6,"stl":1.7,"blk":0.2,"tov":2.2,"fg_pct":0.417,"fg3_pct":0.393,"ft_pct":0.811,"orb":null,"pf":2.4},"tot":{"mp":1221,"fg":207,"fga":496,"fg3":75,"fg3a":191,"ft":146,"fta":180,"orb":null,"drb":null,"trb":92,"ast":132,"stl":63,"blk":7,"tov":82,"pf":87,"pts":635,"g":37},"adv":{"per":null,"ts_pct":0.546,"efg_pct":0.493,"usg_pct":null,"ast_pct":null,"trb_pct":null,"stl_pct":null,"blk_pct":null,"ws":null,"ws_per_40":null,"bpm":null,"obpm":null,"dbpm":null},"verified":["record_book"]},{"pid":"nadav-henefeld-1","name":"Nadav Henefeld","num":